messages.reasoning_content:
The AppShell is `display: flex; flex-direction: column; height: 100vh; overflow: hidden`, and Footer has `margin-top: auto`. So the structure is:

```
<AppShell>            (flex column, 100vh)
  <Header title="Colour Greeting" />
  <main or div>       (content area — needs flex: 1 and centering)
    ...greeting + buttons...
  </main>
  <Footer>...</Footer>
</AppShell>
```

The footer has margin-top: auto which pushes it to the bottom. For the content area, I'll use a `<main>` with flex: 1, display: flex, flex-direction: column, align-items: center, justify-content: center. Then the h1 and a row of buttons with a gap.

Now the greeting color: `color: var(--color-cat-1)` etc. via inline style — inline style with `color: "var(--color-cat-1)"` uses the token, no hex. That's the cleanest: keep selected token name in state.

State: `useState("--color-cat-1")` storing the token name; buttons set it. Colors mapping:
- Blue → --color-cat-1 (default)
- Orange → --color-cat-6
- Violet → --color-cat-5

Files to write:

1. `src/main.jsx` — entry, imports styles once:
```jsx
import { StrictMode } from "react";
import { createRoot } from "react-dom/client";
import "@codesweep-ai/ui/styles/core.css";
import "@codesweep-ai/ui/styles/components/app-shell.css";
import "@codesweep-ai/ui/styles/components/button.css";
import "./index.css";
import App from "./App.jsx";

createRoot(document.getElementById("root")).render(
  <StrictMode>
    <App />
  </StrictMode>
);
```

Wait — brief says "Import the styles once at the entry point" listing the three sheets. My own index.css for layout is separate and fine (the app needs centering CSS; no hex values though — I only use tokens and layout properties).

2. `src/App.jsx`:
```jsx
import { useState } from "react";
import { AppShell, Header, Footer, Button } from "@codesweep-ai/ui";

const COLOURS = [
  { label: "Blue", token: "--color-cat-1" },
  { label: "Orange", token: "--color-cat-6" },
  { label: "Violet", token: "--color-cat-5" },
];

export default function App() {
  const [colour, setColour] = useState(COLOURS[0].token);

  return (
    <AppShell>
      <Header title="Colour Greeting" />
      <main className="content">
        <h1 className="greeting" style={{ color: `var(${colour})` }}>
          Hello, world!
        </h1>
        <div className="button-row">
          {COLOURS.map(({ label, token }) => (
            <Button
              key={token}
              variant="secondary"
              size="md"
              onClick={() => setColour(token)}
            >
              {label}
            </Button>
          ))}
        </div>
      </main>
      <Footer>Built with the codesweep design system</Footer>
    </AppShell>
  );
}
```

3. `src/index.css`:
```css
.content {
  flex: 1;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: var(--space-6, 24px);
}

.greeting {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: ...;
}

.button-row {
  display: flex;
  gap: var(--space-3);
  justify-content: center;
}
```

Hmm, should I use DS tokens like var(--space-*) in my CSS? The tokens exist (core.css holds tokens). Using var(--space-3) etc. is safe with fallbacks. Keep it simple: use token references with fallbacks for gaps. Actually "small even gap between them" — I'll use `var(--space-3, 0.75rem)`. For the h1 "single large heading" — set a large font-size, maybe `var(--text-4xl, 2.25rem)`... I don't know the exact token names. Let me check core.css/tokens.css for font-size tokens quickly. Simpler: just use a plain `font-size: 3rem;`? That's not a color so hex restriction doesn't apply, but let me check if there are font-size tokens to be idiomatic. Let me grep tokens.css for font-size.

Also check core.css includes tokens (does core.css import tokens.css?). The brief says "The core sheet holds the tokens" — so core.css includes the token definitions. Good.

